# Before pushing

**Read `PUSHING.local.md` at the repo root first.** It is gitignored and
machine-specific, and it documents the one step that is not discoverable from the
repository: `origin` is an HTTPS remote under a different GitHub account than the
default one on this machine, so `git push` fails with a 403 until the active account
is switched, and must be switched back afterwards. Switching the remote to SSH does
not work around it.

Never force-push `main` or rewrite pushed history. Never `cargo publish` without
asking: the crate is deliberately unpublished, and publishing makes the CLI flags a
semver surface.

# What CI enforces

CI runs on every push to `main` with `RUSTFLAGS: -D warnings` and
`RUSTDOCFLAGS: -D warnings`, so anything that merely warns locally fails there:

```bash
cargo fmt --all --check
cargo clippy --all-targets --all-features
cargo test --all-features
cargo doc --no-deps --all-features
```

Run `cargo fmt --all` *before* committing. Formatting a file after committing it
leaves the pushed commit failing `fmt --check` on its own, and drags the fix into an
unrelated later commit.

# Changes to pacing, timing or buffering

`cargo test` drives the scheduler on a synthetic clock, so it cannot see arrival
behaviour at all. Buffer sizing, the start gate and stall detection are functions of
*when* packets turn up, so also run the compliance harness, which needs TSDuck
(`tsp`) and `ffmpeg` on `PATH`:

```bash
./test/run.sh --bursty
```
